<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Psusennes II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pasebakhaenniut II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Psusennes II was the last king of the Twenty-first Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning in the mid-10th century BCE. He may also have held the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, uniting the northern kingship and the Theban priesthood. On his death the throne passed peacefully to his relative by marriage, the Libyan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a>, founder of the Twenty-second Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii.jpg" alt="Psusennes II" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Legrain (1865-1917) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_CG42192_legrain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Psusennes II, whose Egyptian name was Pasebakhaennuit and throne name Titkheperure, was the son of the High Priest of Amun Pinedjem II and of Istemkheb. His parents were siblings, children of the High Priest of Amun Menkheperre, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i">Pinedjem I</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a> was also a son of Pinedjem I. A daughter of Psusennes II, Maatkare, became Great Royal Wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-i">Osorkon I</a> of the next dynasty. When Pinedjem II died in the tenth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siamun">Siamun</a>, a Psusennes, commonly thought identical with Psusennes II, succeeded him as High Priest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, and so became the de facto ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Psusennes II was neither a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a> nor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siamun">Siamun</a>, but succeeded his cousin Siamun under uncertain circumstances. A graffito in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a>'s temple at Abydos gives the complete titles of a king Pasebakhaenniut who is also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> and supreme military commander. The Egyptologist Karl Jansen-Winkeln reads this as showing that he ruled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> and held the Theban priesthood at once. Few records of his reign survive, though a gilded coffin with a royal uraeus, found in the antechamber of the tomb of Psusennes I at Tanis, is presumed to be his burial.</p><p>The length of his reign is debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>'s Epitome gives him 14 or 35 years, usually amended to 15. A block of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> priestly annals, published by Frédéric Payraudeau, records a year 11 of a king Psusennes and is the first secure date for the reign. Aidan Dodson then abandoned his earlier view that the reign lay within that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a>. Rolf Krauss argues for 24 years, from a land register of a year 19 of Pharaoh Psusennes cited in the Large Dakhla stela of year 5 of Shoshenq I, and the editors of the Handbook of Ancient Egyptian Chronology of 2006 adopted a reign of 967 to 943 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His original royal tomb has never been found. It has been proposed that he is one of two decayed mummies in the antechamber of the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>, identified by shabtis bearing his name, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flooded his own tomb and forced a reburial there. The Twenty-first Dynasty ended with him, and his name appears alongside that of his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> on a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> and in a graffito from Theban Tomb 18.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Psusennes II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 943 BCE Sheshonq I, a Libyan chief of the Meshwesh and relative by marriage of the last king, <b>Psusennes II</b>, succeeded peacefully and founded the Twenty-second Dynasty of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
